The Schedule to the Act contains the provisions of the Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations, 1961. The Convention sets out the rules governing diplomatic relations between States, including the privileges and immunities of diplomatic missions and their members. The Convention consists of 53 articles, which cover various aspects of diplomatic relations, such as the functions and duties of diplomatic missions, the inviolability of diplomatic agents and their premises, and the privileges and immunities of diplomatic agents and their families.

The Convention also provides for the waiver of immunity by the sending State, the exemption of diplomatic agents from social security provisions, and the exemption of diplomatic agents from dues and taxes. The Convention also sets out the rules governing the entry and departure of diplomatic agents, the transit of diplomatic agents through third States, and the settlement of disputes between States.

In summary, the Diplomatic Relations (Vienna Convention) Act, 1972, provides for the implementation of the Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations, 1961, in India. The Act sets out the privileges and immunities of diplomatic missions and their members, and provides for the waiver of immunity and the exemption of diplomatic agents from social security provisions and dues and taxes. The Act also sets out the rules governing the entry and departure of diplomatic agents, the transit of diplomatic agents through third States, and the settlement of disputes between States. The Schedule to the Act contains the provisions of the Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations, 1961.
