The Hazardous and Other Wastes (Management and Transboundary Movement) Rules, 2016, is a set of rules that govern the management of hazardous and other wastes in India. These rules apply to the management of hazardous and other wastes as specified in the Schedules to these rules, but do not apply to waste-water and exhaust gases, wastes arising out of the operation from ships beyond five kilometers of the relevant baseline, radio-active wastes, bio-medical wastes, and municipal solid wastes.
1. Form No. 25: This is a Magistrate's Order prohibiting processions on a specific street. The Magistrate issues this order to prevent a breach of the peace. The order prohibits any procession from passing along the street and warns individuals not to take part in such a procession.
2. Form No. 25: This is a Magistrate's Order declaring a party entitled to retain possession of land or other property in dispute. The Magistrate issues this order after conducting an inquiry into the dispute and being satisfied that one party is in actual possession of the property. The Magistrate declares that the party is entitled to retain possession until ousted by due course of law.
3. Form No. 26: This is a Warrant of Attachment in the case of a dispute as to the possession of land or other property. The Magistrate issues this warrant when a dispute is likely to induce a breach of the peace, and the parties cannot agree on who is in possession of the property. The warrant authorizes the police to take possession of the property and hold it under attachment until the decree or order of a competent court determines the rights of the parties.
4. Form No. 27: This is a Magistrate's Order prohibiting the doing of anything on land or water. The Magistrate issues this order when a dispute arises concerning the right of use of land or water. The Magistrate orders that the claimant or claimants of possession shall not take possession of the land or water to the exclusion of the enjoyment of the right of use.
5. Form No. 28: This is a Bond and Bail-Bond on a preliminary inquiry before a police officer. The accused person signs this bond, agreeing to appear before the Magistrate when required. If the accused fails to appear, they bind themselves to forfeit a specified sum of money to the Government.
6. Form No. 29: This is a Bond to prosecute or give evidence. The person who signs this bond agrees to attend court and prosecute or give evidence in a specific case. If they fail to appear, they bind themselves to forfeit a specified sum of money to the Government.
7. Form No. 30: This is a Special Summons to a person accused of a petty offence. The Magistrate issues this summons, requiring the accused to appear in court on a specific date and time. The summons also provides the accused with the option to plead guilty without appearing in court.
8. Form No. 31: This is a Notice of Commitment by Magistrate to Public Prosecutor. The Magistrate issues this notice, instructing the Public Prosecutor to conduct the prosecution of a specific case.
9. Form No. 32: This is a Charges sheet. The Magistrate issues this sheet, outlining the charges against the accused. The sheet includes the name of the accused, the offence, and the section of the Indian Penal Code that the offence falls under.

These forms and orders are used in various stages of the legal process in India, from preliminary inquiries to trials. They provide a standardized way of handling legal disputes and ensure that the legal process is fair and transparent.
