(2) Where the debtor is a partner of a firm, such debtor shall not apply under this Chapter to the Adjudicating Authority in respect of the firm unless all or a majority of the partners of the firm file the application jointly.

(3) An application under sub-section (1) shall be submitted only in respect of debts which are not excluded debts.

(4) A debtor shall not be entitled to make an application under sub-section (1) if he is—

(a) an undischarged bankrupt;

(b) undergoing a fresh start process;

(c) undergoing an insolvency resolution process under this Code; or

(d) has been adjudged as an insolvent under the Companies Act, 2013.

(5) An application under sub-section (1) shall contain the following details, namely:—

(a) the name of the debtor, his contact details, and the nature of the business carried on by him;

(b) the total debt of the debtor and the amount of default;

(c) the particulars of the creditors, including their names, addresses, and the amounts due to them;

(d) the particulars of the security interest, if any, in favour of any creditor;

(e) the names and contact details of the resolution professional proposed to be appointed under section 95;

(f) the list of assets and liabilities of the debtor, including their current valuation;

(g) the cash flow statement of the debtor for the last two years;

(h) the details of any litigation or legal proceedings initiated or pending against the debtor;

(i) the names and contact details of the auditor and the legal adviser of the debtor; and

(j) any other information that the debtor may consider relevant for the purposes of the insolvency resolution process.

(6) The debtor shall submit the application under sub-section (1) with such fee as may be prescribed.

(7) The Adjudicating Authority may require the debtor to submit any additional information or document that it deems necessary for the purposes of considering the application.

Section 95: Appointment of interim resolution professional

(1) The Adjudicating Authority shall, within fourteen days of the receipt of the application under section 94, by an order—

(a) admit the application, if it is complete in all respects; or

(b) reject the application, if it is incomplete or contains any deficiency.

(2) Where the application is admitted under sub-section (1), the Adjudicating Authority shall, by the same order, appoint an interim resolution professional for carrying out the functions as specified in section 11.

(3) The interim resolution professional shall, within seven days of his appointment, take charge of the assets of the debtor and conduct an initial assessment of the debtor's assets and liabilities.

(4) The interim resolution professional shall, within ten days of his appointment, issue a public announcement inviting claims from the creditors.

(5) The interim resolution professional shall constitute a committee of creditors within thirty days of his appointment.

(6) The interim resolution professional shall submit a report to the Adjudicating Authority on the feasibility and viability of the insolvency resolution process within forty-five days of his appointment.

(7) The interim resolution professional shall perform such other functions as may be specified by the Adjudicating Authority.

Section 96: Moratorium

(1) On the admission of an application under section 94, the Adjudicating Authority shall, by order, declare a moratorium for a period of one hundred and eighty days.

(2) During the moratorium period—

(a) no legal action shall be initiated or continued against the debtor or his property in respect of any debt or obligation;

(b) the transfer or alienation of the debtor's assets shall be stayed;

(c) the debtor shall not be allowed to dispose of or deal with any of his assets or any legal right or beneficial interest in them;

(d) the debtor shall not be allowed to create any security interest in respect of his assets;

(e) the creditor shall not be allowed to enforce any security interest created in respect of the debtor's assets;

(f) the debtor shall not be allowed to make any payment to any creditor in respect of any debt or obligation; and

(g) the debtor shall not be allowed to make any payment in respect of any outstanding amount to any financial creditor in respect of a financial debt.

(3) The moratorium shall not apply to—

(a) such transactions as may be specified by the Central Government in consultation with any financial sector regulator;

(b) the recovery of any property by an owner or lessor where such property is in the possession of the debtor;

(c) the creation or enforcement of any security interest in respect of any asset acquired after the commencement of the moratorium; and

(d) the creation and enforcement of any security interest in respect of any asset other than an asset covered under clause (b) of sub-section (2), where such security interest is in favour of a financial creditor.

