The fifth section, No. 12 DECREE FOR RE-CONVEYANCE OR RE-TRANSFER IN CASE OF ANOMALOUS MORTGAGE (Order XXXIV, rule 9), explains the process for re-conveying or re-transferring a property in the case of an anomalous mortgage, where the mortgage-deed only provides for foreclosure and not sale. The court can order that the defendants be debarred from redeeming the mortgaged property, and that they deliver up possession of the property to the plaintiff.

Overall, these legal orders and decrees provide a framework for resolving property disputes in India, particularly those involving mortgaged properties. They outline the processes for recovering balances due, foreclosing on properties, selling properties, and re-conveying or re-transferring properties after the amounts due have been paid.
Summary:
This document is a preliminary decree for the redemption of a prior mortgage and foreclosure or sale on a subsequent mortgage. It is ordered and decreed that the amount due to the second defendant on the mortgage mentioned in the plaint is the sum of Rs. X for principal, Rs. Y for interest, Rs. Z for costs, and Rs. W for the costs of the suit. The second defendant is entitled to payment of the amount due to them in priority to the plaintiff. The plaintiff or defendant No. 1 must pay into court the amount due to defendant No. 2 on or before a specified date. Defendant No. 2 must bring into court all documents relating to the mortgaged property and deliver them over to the plaintiff or defendant No. 1 on payment of the amount due. If there is a default in payment, defendant No. 2 may apply to the court for dismissal of the suit or a final decree for foreclosure or sale of the mortgaged property. The money realized from the sale will be applied in payment of the amounts due to defendant No. 2 and the plaintiff, and any balance will be paid to defendant No. 1 or other persons entitled to receive the same. If the money realized is not sufficient for payment in full, defendant No. 2 or the plaintiff may apply for a personal decree against defendant No. 1 for the amounts remaining due.
Summary:
This document outlines various legal orders and decrees related to Indian laws. The first decree is a preliminary decree for sale in a mortgage suit between a sub or derivative mortgagee (plaintiff) and a mortgagor and original mortgagee (defendants 1 and 2). The decree declares the amount due to defendant 2 on their mortgage and orders defendant 1 to pay that amount into court. If defendant 2 pays the amount due to the plaintiff, the plaintiff must bring all documents related to the mortgaged property into court, and defendant 2 must re-convey or re-transfer the property to defendant 1 free of encumbrances and liabilities. Upon payment of the amount due to defendant 2 by defendant 1, the plaintiff may apply for payment of the sum due to them, and any balance shall be paid to defendant 2. If the amount paid into court is not sufficient to pay the plaintiff in full, the plaintiff may apply for a personal decree against defendant 2 for the balance. The decree also orders that if defendant 2 pays the amount due to the plaintiff, they must bring all documents related to the mortgaged property into court. If defendant Nos. 1 and 2 do not pay as ordered, the plaintiff may apply for a final decree for sale, and the mortgaged property shall be sold to pay the amounts due to the plaintiff and defendant 2. If the money realized by such sale is not sufficient to pay the amounts due in full, the plaintiff or defendant 2 may apply for a personal decree against the other for the balance. The decree also orders that if defendant 2 pays the amount due to the plaintiff but defendant 1 does not pay the amount due to defendant 2, defendant 2 may apply for a final decree for foreclosure or sale. The parties are at liberty to apply to the court as they may have occasion, and the court may give such directions as it thinks fit. The document also includes decrees for rectification of instrument, setting aside a transfer in fraud of creditors, injunctions against private nuisance, building higher than the old level, and restraining the use of a private road. The document also includes a preliminary decree in an administration-suit, ordering accounts and inquiries to be taken and made.
Summary:
This document presents a series of legal orders and instructions for dealing with a deceased person's estate in India. The orders cover various aspects of the estate, including the distribution of assets, payment of debts, and the appointment of a receiver.

