Summary:

THE ENVIRONMENT (PROTECTION) ACT, 1986, is an Act designed to protect and improve the environment and prevent, control, and abate environmental pollution. The Act empowers the Central Government to take all necessary measures to protect and improve the environment, including laying down standards for the quality of the environment and emission or discharge of environmental pollutants, restricting areas where certain industries, operations, or processes can be carried out, and establishing procedures and safeguards for the prevention of accidents that may cause environmental pollution. The Act also imposes penalties for violations of its provisions.

Key provisions of the Act include:

* The Central Government has the power to take all necessary measures to protect and improve the environment, including laying down standards for the quality of the environment and emission or discharge of environmental pollutants, restricting areas where certain industries, operations, or processes can be carried out, and establishing procedures and safeguards for the prevention of accidents that may cause environmental pollution.
* Persons carrying on any industry, operation, or process are prohibited from discharging or emitting any environmental pollutant in excess of the standards prescribed.
* Persons handling hazardous substances must comply with prescribed procedures and safeguards.
* Persons responsible for the discharge of environmental pollutants in excess of the prescribed standards must take remedial measures to prevent or mitigate environmental pollution and inform the authorities or agencies prescribed.
* The Central Government or any officer empowered by it has the power to take samples of air, water, soil, or other substances for analysis, and the results of such analysis are admissible as evidence in legal proceedings.
* Environmental laboratories are established or recognized by the Central Government to carry out the functions entrusted to them under this Act.
* Government Analysts are appointed or recognized by the Central Government to analyze samples of air, water, soil, or other substances sent for analysis to any environmental laboratory.
* Penalties are imposed for violations of the Act, including imprisonment for a term that may extend to five years or a fine that may extend to one lakh rupees, or both, and additional fines for continuing violations.
* Companies and Government Departments are held liable for offenses committed by them.
* No suit, prosecution, or other legal proceedings shall lie against the Government or any officer or other employee of the Government or any authority constituted under this Act or any member, officer, or other employee of such authority in respect of anything which is done or intended to be done in good faith in pursuance of this Act or the rules made or orders or directions issued thereunder.
* The Central Government may, by notification in the Official Gazette, delegate its powers and functions under this Act to any officer, State Government, or other authority, subject to such conditions and limitations as may be specified in the notification.
* The provisions of this Act and the rules or orders made therein shall have effect notwithstanding anything inconsistent therewith contained in any enactment other than this Act.
* Every rule made under this Act shall be laid, as soon as may be after it is made, before each House of Parliament, while it is in session, for a total period of thirty days which may be comprised in one session or in two or more successive sessions.
(a) "Act" means the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 (29 of 1986);

(b) "authentic record" means a document containing information about the generation, storage, treatment, transportation, import and export, recycling, recovery or disposal of hazardous and other wastes, which is duly authenticated by the generator, owner or operator, as the case may be;

(c) "Board" means the Central Pollution Control Board constituted under section 3 of the Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 (6 of 1974);

(d) "competent authority" means the State Pollution Control Board or the Pollution Control Committee of the Union territory, as the case may be, constituted under section 4 of the Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 (6 of 1974);

(e) "disposal" means the final disposal of hazardous and other wastes by any method, including dumping in land, sea or air, that results in the loss of the wastes or any of its components;

(f) "environment" includes water, air and land and the inter-relationship which exists among and between water, air and land and human beings, other living creatures, plants, micro-organisms and property;

(g) "generator" means any person who produces hazardous and other wastes as specified in the Schedules to these rules;

(h) "handler" means any person who stores, treats, recycles, reuses, transports, imports, exports or disposes of hazardous and other wastes as specified in the Schedules to these rules;

(i) "hazardous waste" means any waste as specified in Column 3 of Schedule I;

(j) "import" means bringing into India from a place outside India;

(k) "inspector" means any person authorized by the Central Government or the State Government or the Central Pollution Control Board or the State Pollution Control Board or the Pollution Control Committee of the Union territory to inspect any premises, vehicle, vessel, aircraft, installation or other place where hazardous and other wastes are generated, treated, stored, transported, imported, exported, recycled, recovered or disposed of or for taking samples of such wastes for the purpose of examination, analysis or testing;

(l) "occupier" means every person who has control over and is responsible for the conduct of any industry, operation, or process or for the condition or maintenance of any premises or for the disposal of any waste;

(m) "owner" means a person who owns, holds or enjoys any premises or has the control over the affairs of the premises;

(n) "prescribed" means prescribed by rules made under the Act;

(o) "recycling" means any operation the primary purpose of which is to recover a waste and to reuse that waste without any other pre-processing, except for the removal of impurities;

(p) "recovery" means any operation the primary purpose of which is to extract or remove valuable components from waste, which can be subsequently reused without any other pre-processing;

(q) "Schedule" means a Schedule appended to these rules;

(r) "Schedule I" means the Schedule appended to these rules and relating to hazardous wastes;

(s) "Schedule II" means the Schedule appended to these rules and relating to other wastes;

(t) "State Government" means the Government of any State of the Union of India;

(u) "storage" means the holding of hazardous and other wastes for a temporary period, pending their treatment, recycling, reuse, recovery, disposal or export;

(v) "treatment" means any method, technique or process, including neutralization, designed to change the physical, chemical or biological character or composition of any hazardous and other wastes to render it safer for transport, storage, recycling, reuse, recovery, disposal or export or for reducing the volume of such wastes;

(w) "transboundary movement" means any movement of hazardous and other wastes from an area under the national jurisdiction of one country to or through an area under the national jurisdiction of another country or to or through the high seas;

(x) "transport" means the movement of hazardous and other wastes by road, rail, sea, air or pipeline from one place to another;

(y) "vehicle" means any conveyance used for the transport of hazardous and other wastes by road or rail;

(z) "waste" means any substance, whether gaseous, liquid or solid, which is discharged, discarded or abandoned by the person generating it, and is not covered under the categories mentioned in clauses (a) to (e) of sub-rule (1) of rule 2.


**Summary:**

The Hazardous and Other Wastes (Management and Transboundary Movement) Rules, 2016, is a set of rules that govern the management of hazardous and other wastes in India. These rules apply to the management of hazardous and other wastes as specified in the Schedules to these rules, but do not apply to waste-water and exhaust gases, wastes arising out of the operation from ships beyond five kilometers of the relevant baseline, radio-active wastes, bio-medical wastes, and municipal solid wastes.

Section 120 provides that when the Court has to form an opinion as to the contents of any document, other than a document produced in evidence in the case, the Court may either take notice of the contents of such document without proof, or may require evidence to be given of the contents of such document, as it thinks fit.

Section 121 provides that when the Court has to form an opinion as to the meaning of any written or printed word or symbol, the Court may either take notice of that meaning without proof, or may require evidence to be given of that meaning, as it thinks fit.

Section 122 provides that the Court shall take judicial notice of the following facts, namely:

(a) the day of the week on which any day of the year falls;

(b) the month in which any day of the year falls;

(c) the date of any public Act, Ordinance, Regulation, Notification, or other official document;

(d) the contents of any published document declared by this Act to be admissible in evidence;

(e) the signatures of any well-known person, or of any person in public or private service, or of any person in the regular course of business, or of any person in any professional capacity;

(f) the contents of any document forming part of the records of any Court, or of any public office or registry, or of any public or local fund, or of any Court, public office, registry or fund in any foreign country;

(g) the fact that any document is registered, or that any person is registered as the owner, executor, administrator or assignee of any property, or as the proprietor of any patent or trademark, or as the holder of any license, or as a member of any chamber of commerce or of any trade association;

(h) the identity and genuineness of handwriting, signatures, seals and finger impressions;

(i) the general course of nature;

(j) the course of business;

(k) the situation and extent of any country, state, city, town, village or other place;

(l) the boundaries and areas of any electoral constituency;

(m) the names of the members of the Cabinet and of the Council of Ministers of the Union and of the members of the Council of Ministers of any State;

(n) the names of the Presidents, Vice-Presidents, Governors, Lieutenant-Governors, Chief Justices, Judges and other officers of the Union and of the several States;

(o) the names of the members of the two Houses of Parliament and of the members of the two Houses of the Legislature of any State, and the names of the persons holding office as such members;

(p) the names of the members of the Armed Forces of the Union and of the police forces of the Union and of the several States;

(q) the names of the members of the Civil Services of the Union and of the several States;

(r) the names of the members of the Judicial Services of the Union and of the several States;

(s) the names of the persons holding any public office;

(t) the names of the members of any foreign legislature, and of the members of any foreign Cabinet or of any foreign Council of Ministers;

(u) the names of the members of the Executive and of the Legislature of any foreign country;

(v) the names of the members of any international body;

(w) the names of the members of any local authority;

(x) the names of the members of any university, college or school;

(y) the names of the persons holding any appointment in any university, college or school;

(z) the names of the members of any club, society or association;

