Rule 43A allows for the custody of movable property to be left in the village or place where it was attached, in the custody of a respectable person (the "custodian"). If the custodian fails to produce the property at the designated place or time, or if the property is not in the same condition as when it was entrusted to them, they may be liable to pay compensation to the decree-holder, judgment-debtor, or any other person found to be entitled to the restoration of the property.

Rule 44 states that the attachment of agricultural produce must be made by affixing a copy of the warrant of attachment to the land on which the crop has grown, or to the threshing floor or other place where the produce has been cut or gathered. The produce is then deemed to have passed into the possession of the Court.

Rule 45 outlines provisions for the custody of agricultural produce under attachment, including arrangements for its custody and the ability of the judgment-debtor to tend, cut, gather, and store the produce. If the judgment-debtor fails to do so, the decree-holder may do so with the permission of the Court and the costs incurred by the decree-holder shall be recoverable from the judgment-debtor.

Rule 46 states that in the case of a debt not secured by a negotiable instrument, a share in the capital of a corporation, or other movable property not in the possession of the judgment-debtor (except property deposited in, or in the custody of, any Court), the attachment shall be made by a written order prohibiting the debtor from paying the debt, the person in whose name the share may be standing from transferring the same or receiving any dividend thereon, or the person in possession of the same from giving it over to the judgement-debtor.

Rule 46A allows for the Court to issue notice to the garnishee liable to pay the debt, calling upon them to pay the debt due to the judgment-debtor or show cause why they should not do so. If the garnishee fails to comply with the notice, the Court may order them to comply and execution may issue as though the order were a decree against them.

Rule 46B outlines the procedure for trying disputed questions where the garnishee disputes liability. If the debt in respect of which the application under rule 46A is made is beyond the pecuniary jurisdiction of the Court, the Court shall send the execution case to the Court of the District Judge to which the said Court is subordinate.

Rule 46D states that where it is suggested or appears to be probable that the debt belongs to some third person, or that any third person has a lien or charge on, or other interest in such debt, the Court may order such third person to appear and state the nature and particulars of his claim, if any, to such debt and prove the same.

Rule 46F states that payment made by the garnishee on notice under rule 46A or under any such order as aforesaid shall be a valid discharge to him as against the judgement-debtor and any other person ordered to appear as aforesaid for the amount paid or levied, although the decree in execution of which the application under rule 46A was made, or the order passed in the proceedings on such application may be set aside or reversed.

Rule 46G states that the costs of any application made under rule 46A and of any proceeding arising therefrom or incidental thereto shall be in the discretion of the Court.

Rule 46H states that an order made under rule 46B, rule 46C or rule 46E shall be applicable as a decree.
