Summary:

The document contains information about various Indian laws related to prosecution and complaints. Section 198 of the Indian Penal Code (IPC) explains the provisions for prosecution for offences against marriage. Only a person aggrieved by the offence can make a complaint, with some exceptions. For instance, if the person is underage, a guardian can make a complaint on their behalf. Similarly, if the person is the wife, her relatives can make a complaint on her behalf. Section 198A of the IPC deals with the prosecution of offences under section 498A of the IPC, which pertains to cruelty by a husband or his relatives. A complaint can be made by the wife or her relatives. Section 198B of the IPC deals with the cognizance of offences under section 376B of the IPC, which pertains to sexual intercourse by a man with his wife during separation. A complaint can be made by the wife against the husband. Section 199 of the IPC deals with the prosecution for defamation. A complaint can be made by the person aggrieved by the offence, and in certain cases, the Public Prosecutor can make a complaint with the previous sanction of the State or Central Government. Chapter XV of the Code of Criminal Procedure (CrPC) deals with complaints to Magistrates. Section 200 of the CrPC explains the examination of the complainant by a Magistrate taking cognizance of an offence on complaint. The Magistrate shall examine the complainant and the witnesses present and reduce the substance of such examination to writing, which shall be signed by the complainant, the witnesses, and the Magistrate. However, if the complaint is made in writing, the Magistrate need not examine the complainant and the witnesses if the complaint is made by a public servant acting or purporting to act in the discharge of his official duties or a Court or if the Magistrate makes over the case for inquiry or trial to another Magistrate under section 192. Section 201 of the CrPC deals with the procedure by a Magistrate not competent to take cognizance of the case. If the complaint is made to a Magistrate who is not competent to take cognizance of the offence, he shall return it for presentation to the proper Court or direct the complainant to the proper Court. Section 202 of the CrPC deals with postponement of issue of process. Any Magistrate, on receipt of a complaint, may postpone the issue of process against the accused and either inquire into the case himself or direct an investigation to be made by a police officer or by such other person as he thinks fit for the purpose of deciding whether or not there is sufficient ground for proceeding.
Summary:
The document outlines various legal procedures and provisions under the Indian Penal Code related to the role of a Magistrate in criminal proceedings. Specifically, it discusses the powers and duties of a Magistrate in relation to the investigation and inquiry of a case, the issuance of summons and warrants, and the supply of copies of police reports and other documents to the accused. The document also includes provisions related to the commitment of a case to the Court of Session when the offence is triable exclusively by it, as well as the procedure to be followed when there is a complaint case and police investigation in respect of the same offence. Throughout the document, various legal terms and concepts are used, such as summons-case, warrant-case, process-fees, personal attendance of the accused, petty offence, and police report. These terms are clearly defined and explained in the document, making it accessible to a general audience. Overall, the document provides a comprehensive overview of the legal procedures and provisions related to the role of a Magistrate in criminal proceedings under Indian law.
