Summary:

THE SUITS VALUATION ACT, 1887, is an Act that prescribes the mode of valuing certain suits for the purpose of determining the jurisdiction of courts with respect to them. The Act has three parts.

Part I, titled "SUITS RELATING TO LAND," applies to specific local areas, and its provisions come into force in those areas on dates determined by the State Government. The State Government has the power to make rules for determining the value of land for jurisdictional purposes in suits mentioned in the Court-fees Act, 1870. The rules can determine the value of any class of land or interest in land in the whole or any part of a local area and may prescribe different values for different places within the same local area. The amount at which relief sought in a suit relating to land or interest in land is valued for jurisdictional purposes cannot exceed the value of the land or interest as determined by those rules. The State Government must consult the High Court before making rules under section 3, and a rule shall not take effect till the expiration of one month after the rule has been published in the Official Gazette.

Part II, titled "OTHER SUITS," applies to suits other than those referred to in the Court-fees Act, 1870, section 7, paragraphs v, vi, and ix, and paragraph x, clause (d). Where court-fees are payable ad valorem under the Court-fees Act, 1870, the value as determinable for the computation of court-fees and the value for purposes of jurisdiction shall be the same. When the subject-matter of suits of any class does not admit of being satisfactorily valued, the High Court may direct that such suits shall, for the purposes of the Court-fees Act, 1870, and this Act, be treated as if their subject-matter were of such value as the High Court thinks fit to specify in this behalf.

Part III, titled "SUPPLEMENTAL PROVISIONS," lays down the procedure for dealing with objections regarding the over-valuation or under-valuation of a suit or appeal. The objection must be taken in the Court of first instance at or before the hearing at which issues are first framed and recorded, or in the lower Appellate Court in the memorandum of appeal to that Court. The Appellate Court may entertain the objection if it is satisfied that the suit or appeal was over-valued or under-valued and that the over-valuation or under-valuation thereof has prejudicially affected the disposal of the suit or appeal on its merits. The provisions of this section shall apply to a Court exercising revisional jurisdiction under section 622 of the Code of Civil Procedure or other enactment for the time being in force.

The Act applies to the whole of India except the territories which, immediately before the 1st November, 1956, were comprised in Part B States. The Act has been amended in several states, and its provisions have been extended to various Union territories. The Act has been repealed in its application to certain areas.

Summary:
The Bombay Civil Courts’ Act, 1869, is an Act to consolidate and amend the law relating to the District and Subordinate Civil Courts in the Presidency of Bombay. The Act extends to the territories under the Government of the Governor of Bombay in Council in which the Code of Civil Procedure is now in force. The Act is divided into nine parts. Part I is the preliminary part, which includes the short title and extent of the Act. Part II deals with Districts and Sadr Stations, where the Governor of Bombay in Council may alter the limits of existing Zilas (Districts) and create new Districts for the purposes of this Act. Part III deals with District Courts, where there shall be in each District a District Court presided over by a Judge to be called the District Judge. The District Judge shall be appointed by the Governor of Bombay in Council and shall be liable to be suspended or removed from his appointment only by the Governor of Bombay in Council. The District Court shall be the principal Court of original civil jurisdiction in the District, within the meaning of the Code of Civil Procedure. Part IV deals with Joint Judges, where the Governor of Bombay in Council may, with the previous sanction of the Governor General of India in Council, appoint in any District a Joint Judge who shall be invested with co-extensive powers and a concurrent jurisdiction with the District Judge. Part V deals with Assistant Judges, where the Governor of Bombay in Council, under the general control of the Governor General of India in Council, may appoint one or more Assistants to the District Judge. Part VI deals with Subordinate Judges, where the Governor of Bombay in Council, under the general control of the Governor General of India in Council, may appoint one or more Assistants to the District Judge. Part VII deals with Temporary vacancies, where in the event of the death of the District Judge or of his being prevented from performing his duties by illness or other casualty, or of his absence from his District on leave, the first in rank of the Assistant Judges in the District, or in the absence from the District of an Assistant Judge the first in rank of the Subordinate Judges, shall assume charge of the District Court without interruption to his ordinary jurisdiction. Part VIII deals with Ministerial Officers, where all ministerial officers of the Civil Courts in each District shall be appointed, and may be fined, suspended or dismissed by the District Judge, subject to such rules as the High Court may from time to time prescribe. Part IX deals with Miscellaneous, where the proceedings of each Civil Court shall be kept and recorded according to such rules as the High Court may from time to time prescribe. The High Court shall also lay down rules under which copies of papers may be granted. The Act also provides for the fees to be taken for any process issued by any Court the constitution of which is declared by this Act, or by any officer of such Court. The District and Subordinate Courts shall sit from day to day, except on Sundays, New Year's Day, Good Friday, Christmas Day, and Her Majesty’s Birth Day, and such other days as may be sanctioned for each or every District by the High Court. The High Court may also permit the Civil Courts under its control to adjourn for a period or periods not exceeding in the whole six weeks in each year.
Section 120 provides that when the Court has to form an opinion as to the contents of any document, other than a document produced in evidence in the case, the Court may either take notice of the contents of such document without proof, or may require evidence to be given of the contents of such document, as it thinks fit.

Section 121 provides that when the Court has to form an opinion as to the meaning of any written or printed word or symbol, the Court may either take notice of that meaning without proof, or may require evidence to be given of that meaning, as it thinks fit.

