This document discusses the power of the Revisional Authority in India's goods and services tax (GST) system to review decisions made by subordinate officers. The Revisional Authority has the power to stay the operation of any decision or order passed under the GST Act or the State Goods and Services Tax Act or the Union Territory Goods and Services Tax Act if it is erroneous and prejudicial to the interest of revenue. The Revisional Authority can also enhance, modify, or annul the decision or order after giving the person concerned an opportunity of being heard and making further inquiry. However, the Revisional Authority cannot exercise its power if the order has been subject to an appeal, the period specified for appeal has not yet expired or more than three years have expired after the passing of the decision or order, the order has already been taken for revision under this section at an earlier stage, or the order has been passed in exercise of the powers under sub-section (1). The Revisional Authority can pass an order on any point which has not been raised and decided in an appeal before the expiry of a period of one year from the date of the order in such appeal or before the expiry of a period of three years referred to in clause (b) of that sub-section, whichever is later.

The document also discusses the constitution of the Goods and Services Tax Appellate Tribunal (GSTAT) for hearing appeals against the orders passed by the Appellate Authority or the Revisional Authority. The GSTAT shall consist of a National Bench, Regional Benches, State Benches, and Area Benches. The National Bench shall be situated at New Delhi and shall be presided over by the President and shall consist of one Technical Member (Centre) and one Technical Member (State). The Government shall constitute such number of Regional Benches as may be required, and such Regional Benches shall consist of a Judicial Member, one Technical Member (Centre) and one Technical Member (State). The National Bench or Regional Benches of the GSTAT shall have jurisdiction to hear appeals against the orders passed by the Appellate Authority or the Revisional Authority in the cases where one of the issues involved relates to the place of supply. The Government shall specify for each State or Union territory, a Bench of the GSTAT (State Bench) for exercising the powers of the GSTAT within the concerned State or Union territory. The State Bench or Area Benches shall have jurisdiction to hear appeals against the orders passed by the Appellate Authority or the Revisional Authority in the cases involving matters other than those referred to in sub-section (5).

The document also specifies the qualifications, appointment, conditions of service, etc. of the President and Members of the Appellate Tribunal. A person shall not be qualified for appointment as the President unless he has been a Judge of the Supreme Court or is or has been the Chief Justice of a High Court, or is or has been a Judge of a High Court for a period not less than five years. A Judicial Member shall be qualified to be appointed as a Judge of the High Court or is or has been a District Judge qualified to be appointed as a Judge of a High Court or is or has been a Member of Indian Legal Service and has held a post not less than Additional Secretary for three years. A Technical Member (Centre) shall be qualified if he is or has been a member of Indian Revenue (Customs and Central Excise) Service, Group A, and has completed at least fifteen years of service in Group A. A Technical Member (State) shall be qualified if he is or has been an officer of the State Government not below the rank of Additional Commissioner of Value Added Tax or the State goods and services tax or such rank as may be notified by the Government.
Summary:

The document outlines the composition and appointment process for the National Bench, Regional Benches, State Bench, and Area Benches of the Goods and Services Tax Appellate Tribunal (GSTAT) in India. The National Bench and Regional Benches consist of a President and Judicial Members, appointed by the Central Government after consultation with the Chief Justice of India. The Technical Members of the National Bench and Regional Benches are appointed by the Central Government on the recommendations of a Selection Committee. The State Bench and Area Benches consist of a State President and Judicial Members, appointed by the State Government after consultation with the Chief Justice of the High Court of the State. The Technical Members of the State Bench and Area Benches are appointed by the Central Government and State Government, respectively.

The document also specifies the terms and conditions of service for the President, State President, and Members of the GSTAT, including their salaries, allowances, and tenure. It outlines the grounds for removal of the President or Members of the GSTAT, as well as the procedure for suspending and removing them from office.

