(a) "Act" means the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 (29 of 1986);

(b) "authentic record" means a document containing information about the generation, storage, treatment, transportation, import and export, recycling, recovery or disposal of hazardous and other wastes, which is duly authenticated by the generator, owner or operator, as the case may be;

(c) "Board" means the Central Pollution Control Board constituted under section 3 of the Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 (6 of 1974);

(d) "competent authority" means the State Pollution Control Board or the Pollution Control Committee of the Union territory, as the case may be, constituted under section 4 of the Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 (6 of 1974);

(e) "disposal" means the final disposal of hazardous and other wastes by any method, including dumping in land, sea or air, that results in the loss of the wastes or any of its components;

(f) "environment" includes water, air and land and the inter-relationship which exists among and between water, air and land and human beings, other living creatures, plants, micro-organisms and property;

(g) "generator" means any person who produces hazardous and other wastes as specified in the Schedules to these rules;

(h) "handler" means any person who stores, treats, recycles, reuses, transports, imports, exports or disposes of hazardous and other wastes as specified in the Schedules to these rules;

(i) "hazardous waste" means any waste as specified in Column 3 of Schedule I;

(j) "import" means bringing into India from a place outside India;

(k) "inspector" means any person authorized by the Central Government or the State Government or the Central Pollution Control Board or the State Pollution Control Board or the Pollution Control Committee of the Union territory to inspect any premises, vehicle, vessel, aircraft, installation or other place where hazardous and other wastes are generated, treated, stored, transported, imported, exported, recycled, recovered or disposed of or for taking samples of such wastes for the purpose of examination, analysis or testing;

(l) "occupier" means every person who has control over and is responsible for the conduct of any industry, operation, or process or for the condition or maintenance of any premises or for the disposal of any waste;

(m) "owner" means a person who owns, holds or enjoys any premises or has the control over the affairs of the premises;

(n) "prescribed" means prescribed by rules made under the Act;

(o) "recycling" means any operation the primary purpose of which is to recover a waste and to reuse that waste without any other pre-processing, except for the removal of impurities;

(p) "recovery" means any operation the primary purpose of which is to extract or remove valuable components from waste, which can be subsequently reused without any other pre-processing;

(q) "Schedule" means a Schedule appended to these rules;

(r) "Schedule I" means the Schedule appended to these rules and relating to hazardous wastes;

(s) "Schedule II" means the Schedule appended to these rules and relating to other wastes;

(t) "State Government" means the Government of any State of the Union of India;

(u) "storage" means the holding of hazardous and other wastes for a temporary period, pending their treatment, recycling, reuse, recovery, disposal or export;

(v) "treatment" means any method, technique or process, including neutralization, designed to change the physical, chemical or biological character or composition of any hazardous and other wastes to render it safer for transport, storage, recycling, reuse, recovery, disposal or export or for reducing the volume of such wastes;

(w) "transboundary movement" means any movement of hazardous and other wastes from an area under the national jurisdiction of one country to or through an area under the national jurisdiction of another country or to or through the high seas;

(x) "transport" means the movement of hazardous and other wastes by road, rail, sea, air or pipeline from one place to another;

(y) "vehicle" means any conveyance used for the transport of hazardous and other wastes by road or rail;

(z) "waste" means any substance, whether gaseous, liquid or solid, which is discharged, discarded or abandoned by the person generating it, and is not covered under the categories mentioned in clauses (a) to (e) of sub-rule (1) of rule 2.


**Summary:**

The Hazardous and Other Wastes (Management and Transboundary Movement) Rules, 2016, is a set of rules that govern the management of hazardous and other wastes in India. These rules apply to the management of hazardous and other wastes as specified in the Schedules to these rules, but do not apply to waste-water and exhaust gases, wastes arising out of the operation from ships beyond five kilometers of the relevant baseline, radio-active wastes, bio-medical wastes, and municipal solid wastes.
In summary, this document provides a classification system for various chemical compounds under the Harmonized System (HS) of tariff nomenclature. The codes represent specific products or product groups, and each code is followed by a percentage representing the maximum allowed amount of impurities in the product. The document also includes specific codes for certain chemical compounds with a description of the compound.
Summary:

The document presents a list of pharmaceutical products and their respective tariff item descriptions, along with the applicable rates of duty. The list includes various categories of drugs, such as antibiotics, anti-inflammatory agents, and hormonal preparations. The antibiotics category includes Doxycycline and its salts, Tetracycline/oxytetra-cycline and their salts, Chloramphenicol and its derivatives, Erythromycin and its derivatives, Rifampicin and its salts, Cephalexin and its salts, Ciprofloxacine and its salts, Gentamycin and its salts, Neomycin, and Norfloxacin and its salts. The anti-inflammatory agents category includes Ibuprofane, and the hormonal preparations category includes Timolo maleate, Terbutoline sulphate, D(-)phenyl glycin chloride HCL (DPGCH), Imipramine HCl, Amitryptyline HCl, Cysteanune HCl, Atenolol, and Propranalol.

The document also includes a section on "immunological products," which are defined as peptides and proteins directly involved in the regulation of immunological processes, such as monoclonal antibodies, interleukins, interferons, chemokines, tumor necrosis factors, growth factors, hematopoietins, and colony stimulating factors.

The document also specifies certain exclusions from the scope of this chapter, including food or beverages, preparations for assisting smokers to quit smoking, plasters for use in dentistry, aqueous distillates or aqueous solutions of essential oils, preparations of headings 3303 to 3307, soap or other products of heading 3401 containing added medicaments, preparations with a basis of plaster for use in dentistry, and blood albumin not prepared for therapeutic or prophylactic uses.

The document also includes sub-heading notes and tariff item descriptions for human blood, animal blood prepared for therapeutic, prophylactic or diagnostic uses, antisera, other blood fractions and immunological products, whether or not modified or obtained by means of biotechnological processes; vaccines, toxins, cultures of micro-organisms (excluding yeasts) and similar products.

Finally, the document provides the applicable rates of duty for each tariff item, with separate rates for standard and preferential areas.
Summary:

The document provides a detailed list of various pharmaceutical products, their classification, and the corresponding tariff rates under the Customs Tariff Act, 1975. The products are classified based on their composition and intended use, and the tariff rates are expressed as a percentage of the assessable value of the goods. The classification includes several categories of pharmaceutical products, such as vaccines, medicaments, and other pharmaceutical goods.

Under the category of vaccines, there are several sub-categories, including those for tuberculosis, rabies, Japanese encephalitis, whopping cough, and other diseases. The tariff rates for these vaccines range from 10% to 20%. There are also provisions for mixed vaccines, such as DPT, DT, and MMR, with tariff rates ranging from 10% to 20%.

The category of medicaments includes various sub-categories, such as those containing penicillins, streptomycins, antibiotics, and other therapeutic or prophylactic substances. The tariff rates for these products range from 10% to 30%. There are also provisions for medicaments containing hormones, alkaloids, and other products, with tariff rates ranging from 10% to 30%.

The category of other pharmaceutical goods includes several sub-categories, such as human and animal blood, cultures of micro-organisms, toxins, and other products. The tariff rates for these products range from 10% to 30%.

It is important to note that the Customs Tariff Act, 1975, is a federal law in India, and the classification and tariff rates provided in this document are applicable throughout the country. The Act is administered by the Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Customs (CBIC), which is responsible for the collection of customs duties and other indirect taxes. The CBIC is also responsible for the administration of various other laws related to international trade, such as the Foreign Trade (Development and Regulation) Act, 1992, and the Customs Act, 1962.

In this Convention, unless the context otherwise requires:

(a)the term "India" means the territory of India and includes the territorial sea areas adjacent to the territorial waters of India, and such other maritime zone as India may have under international law, and in particular, includes any area adjacent to the territorial waters of India, which may be included in the exclusive economic zone, in accordance with the provisions of sub-section (2) of section 5 of the Territorial Waters, Continental Shelf, Exclusive Economic Zone and other Maritime Zones Act, 1976, and the seabed and subsoil beneath;

(b)the term "Uganda" means the Republic of Uganda and includes the territorial waters adjacent thereto;

(c)the terms "Contracting State" and "the other Contracting State" mean India or Uganda, as the context requires;

(d)the term "person" includes an individual, a company and any other body of persons;

(e)the term "company" means any body corporate or any entity which is treated as a body corporate for tax purposes;

(f)the terms "enterprise of a Contracting State" and "enterprise of the other Contracting State" mean respectively an enterprise carried on by a resident of a Contracting State and an enterprise carried on by a resident of the other Contracting State;

(g)the term "international traffic" means any transport by a ship or aircraft operated by an enterprise of a Contracting State, except when the ship or aircraft is operated solely between places in the other Contracting State;

(h)the term "competent authority" means, in the case of India, the Central Government in the Ministry of Finance (Department of Revenue) or their authorised representative and, in the case of Uganda, the Minister of Finance or his authorised representative;

(i)the term "national" means any individual possessing the nationality or citizenship of a Contracting State;

(j)the term "fiscal year" means:

(i)in the case of India the financial year beginning on the 1st day of April;

(ii)in the case of Uganda, the year beginning on the 1st day of July;

(k)the term "business" includes the performance of professional services and of other activities of an independent character.

Article 4Resident

1. For the purposes of this Convention, the term "resident of a Contracting State" means any person who, under the laws of that State, is liable to tax therein by reason of his domicile, residence, place of management or any other criterion of a similar nature, and also includes that State and any political subdivision or local authority thereof. This term, however, does not include any person who is liable to tax in that State in respect only of income from sources in that State.

2. Where by reason of the provisions of paragraph 1 an individual is a resident of both Contracting States, then his status shall be determined as follows:

(a)he shall be deemed to be a resident of the Contracting State in which he has a permanent home available to him; if he has a permanent home available to him in both Contracting States, he shall be deemed to be a resident of the Contracting State with which his personal and economic relations are closer (centre of vital interests);

(b)if the Contracting State in which he has his centre of vital interests cannot be determined, or if he has not a permanent home available to him in either Contracting State, he shall be deemed to be a resident of the Contracting State in which he has an habitual abode;

(c)if he has an habitual abode in both Contracting States or in neither of them, he shall be deemed to be a resident of the Contracting State of which he is a national;

(d)if he is a national of both Contracting States or of neither of them, the competent authorities of the Contracting States shall settle the question by mutual agreement.

3. Where by reason of the provisions of paragraph 1 a person other than an individual is a resident of both Contracting States, then it shall be deemed to be a resident of the Contracting State in which its place of effective management is situated. If the place of effective management of a person other than an individual cannot be determined, then the competent authorities of the Contracting States shall settle the question by mutual agreement.

Article 5Permanent Establishment

1. For the purposes of this Convention, the term "permanent establishment" means a fixed place of business through which the business of an enterprise is wholly or partly carried on.

2. The term "permanent establishment" includes especially:

(a)a place of management;

(b)a branch;

(c)an office;

(d)a factory;

(e)a workshop;

(f)a sales outlet;

(g)a warehouse in relation to a person providing storage facilities for others;

(h)a farm, plantation or other place where agricultural, forestry, plantation or related activities are carried on;

(i)a mine, an oil or gas well, a quarry or any other place of extraction of natural resources.
The document summarizes provisions related to appointments, terms of office, and powers of the Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson, and members of the Intellectual Property Appellate Board (IPAB) in India.

Appointment of Technical Members requires a minimum of ten years of experience as a tribunal member under the current Act or the Trade and Merchandise Marks Act, 1958, with at least five years in a post not lower than Joint Registrar. Alternatively, a minimum of ten years of experience as an advocate with specialized experience in trade mark law is required. The Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson, and other Members are appointed by the President of India, with the Chairperson's appointment requiring consultation with the Chief Justice of India.

The term of office for the Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson, and Members is five years or until they reach the age of sixty-five (Chairperson and Vice-Chairperson) or sixty-two (Members), whichever comes first. In case of vacancy, absence, illness, or any other cause, the Vice-Chairperson or senior-most Member will act as Chairperson or discharge the functions of the Chairperson.

Salaries, allowances, and other terms and conditions of service for the Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson, and Members are as prescribed. A person who was in government service before assuming office as Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson, or Member is deemed to have retired from service on the date of assuming office.

The Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson, or any other Member may resign by writing to the President of India, with the provision that they will continue to hold office for three months or until a successor takes over. They can only be removed by the President of India on the grounds of proved misbehavior or incapacity after an inquiry by a Judge of the Supreme Court.

The Central Government determines the nature and categories of officers and employees required to assist the IPAB and provides them as needed. The salaries, allowances, and conditions of service of these officers and employees are prescribed.

Any person aggrieved by an order or decision of the Registrar under this Act or the rules made thereunder may prefer an appeal to the IPAB within three months from the date of communication of the order or decision. The IPAB has the power to regulate its own procedure and has the same powers as a civil court for certain matters. No court or other authority shall have jurisdiction over matters referred to in section 91.
Summary:

The Trade Marks Act, 1999, outlines the powers and duties of the Registrar in legal proceedings related to trademarks. According to Section 98, the Registrar has the right to appear and be heard in any legal proceedings before the Appellate Board that involve alteration or rectification of the register or raise questions about the practice of the Trade Marks Registry. The Registrar also has the right to appear in any appeal to the Board from an order of the Registrar on an application for registration of a trade mark that is not opposed or is opposed but considered necessary in the public interest. The Registrar may submit a statement in writing instead of appearing in person. The costs of the Registrar in proceedings before the Appellate Board are at the discretion of the Board and the Registrar shall not be ordered to pay the costs of any of the parties. All cases of appeals against any order or decision of the Registrar and all cases pertaining to rectification of the register, pending before any High Court, shall be transferred to the Appellate Board.

Section 101 defines applying a trade mark or mark or trade description to goods or services. It includes applying it to the goods themselves or using it in relation to services, applying it to any package in or with which the goods are sold, exposed for sale, or had in possession for sale or for any purpose of trade or manufacture, placing, enclosing or annexing any goods which are sold, or exposed for sale, or had in possession for sale or for any purpose of trade or manufacture, in or with any package or other thing to which a trade mark or mark or trade description has been applied, using a trade mark or mark or trade description in any manner reasonably likely to lead to the belief that the goods or services in connection with which it is used are designated or described by that trade mark or mark or trade description, or using a trade mark or trade description in any sign, advertisement, invoice, catalogue, business letter, business paper, price list or other commercial document and goods are delivered or services are rendered to a person in pursuance of a request or order made by reference to the trade mark or trade description as so used.

