Summary:

The Constitution (Forty-Second Amendment) Act, 1976 was enacted to make several changes to the Constitution of India. The Act aimed to emphasize the high ideals of socialism, secularism, and the integrity of the nation, and to make the Directive Principles of State Policy more comprehensive. It also sought to give these principles precedence over fundamental rights that have been used to obstruct socio-economic reforms. The Act also specified the fundamental duties of citizens and made special provisions for dealing with anti-national activities.

Furthermore, the Act proposed to strengthen the presumption in favor of the constitutionality of legislation enacted by Parliament and State Legislatures. It provided for a requirement of a minimum number of Judges to determine questions as to the constitutionality of laws and for a special majority of not less than two-thirds to declare any law to be constitutionally invalid. The Act also took away the jurisdiction of High Courts with regard to determination of Constitutional validity of Central laws and conferred exclusive jurisdiction in this behalf on the Supreme Court.

In addition, the Act proposed to reduce the mounting arrears in High Courts and to secure the speedy disposal of service matters, revenue matters, and certain other matters of special importance. It also made certain modifications in the writ jurisdiction of the High Courts under article 226.

Lastly, the Act made certain other amendments which had become necessary in the light of the working of the Constitution. The various amendments proposed in the Bill were explained in the notes on clauses. The Bill sought to achieve the above objects.

The document contains various legal notices and forms related to Indian laws. The first notice is for the appointment of a guardian for a minor in a civil suit. The notice is given to the minor defendant, requiring them to appear in court and show cause against the application for the appointment of a guardian. If they fail to appear, the application will be heard and determined ex parte. The notice is given under Order XXXII, rule 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908.

The second notice is for the opposite party of a day fixed for hearing evidence of pauperism. The notice is given under Order XXXIII, rule 6 of the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908. It informs the opposite party that a pauper has applied for permission to institute a suit and that a day has been fixed for receiving evidence of the pauper's poverty and for hearing any evidence that may be adduced in disproof thereof. The notice also informs the opposite party that they may offer evidence to disprove the pauper's poverty on appearing in court on the said day.

The third notice is for a surety of their liability under a decree. The notice informs the surety that they are liable for the performance of any decree passed against the defendant in the suit and that an application has been made for the execution of the decree against them. The notice requires the surety to show cause why the decree should not be executed against them and gives them a specified date to do so. If no sufficient cause is shown, an order for execution of the decree will be issued.

The document also contains a Statement of Truth, which is to be used under the First Schedule, Order VI- Rule 15A and Order XI- Rule 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908. The Statement of Truth is an affidavit made by the deponent, who affirms that the statements made in the pleading are true to their knowledge, based on information received or legal advice, and that there is no false statement or concealment of any material fact, document, or record. The deponent also declares that they have disclosed all documents pertaining to the facts and circumstances of the proceedings and that they are aware of the legal consequences of making a false statement or concealment.

The document also includes a State Amendment for Jammu and Kashmir and Ladakh (UTs), which adds a new Appendix-I to the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908. The new Appendix-I contains a Statement of Truth, which is to be used under the First Schedule, Order VI- Rule 15A and Order XI- Rule 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908. The Statement of Truth is an affidavit made by the deponent, who affirms that the statements made in the pleading are true to their knowledge, based on information received or legal advice, and that there is no false statement or concealment of any material fact, document, or record. The deponent also declares that they have disclosed all documents pertaining to the facts and circumstances of the proceedings and that they are aware of the legal consequences of making a false statement or concealment.
