Under Section 28, the arbitral tribunal shall decide the dispute submitted to arbitration in accordance with the substantive law for the time being in force in India in an arbitration other than an international commercial arbitration. In international commercial arbitration, the arbitral tribunal shall decide the dispute in accordance with the rules of law designated by the parties as applicable to the substance of the dispute. The arbitral tribunal shall decide ex aequo et bono or as amiable compositeur only if the parties have expressly authorized it to do so.

Under Section 29, in arbitral proceedings with more than one arbitrator, any decision of the arbitral tribunal shall be made by a majority of all its members. Questions of procedure may be decided by the presiding arbitrator if authorized by the parties or all the members of the arbitral tribunal.

Under Section 29A, the award in matters other than international commercial arbitration shall be made by the arbitral tribunal within a period of twelve months from the date of completion of pleadings under sub-section (4) of section 23. The parties may, by consent, extend the period specified for making the award for a further period not exceeding six months. If the award is not made within the period specified or the extended period, the mandate of the arbitrator(s) shall terminate unless the Court has extended the period. The extension of period may be on the application of any of the parties and may be granted only for sufficient cause and on such terms and conditions as may be imposed by the Court.

Under Section 30, the arbitral award shall be made in writing and shall be signed by the arbitrator or arbitrators. The award shall state the reasons upon which it is based, unless the parties have agreed that no reasons are to be given or the award is an arbitral award on agreed terms under Section 30(4). The award shall state its date and the place of arbitration as determined in accordance with Section 20(1). If the arbitral tribunal consists of more than one arbitrator, the signature of the majority of all the members of the arbitral tribunal shall be sufficient for the award, provided that the reason for any omitted signature is stated.

Under Section 31, the arbitral award shall be final and binding on the parties and persons claiming under them respectively. The award shall be enforceable under the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908 in the same manner as if it were a decree of the court.

Under Section 32, an application for the setting aside of an arbitral award may be made by any party to the arbitration on any of the following grounds, namely: (a) that the parties to the agreement referred to in Section 7 were under some incapacity, or the agreement is not valid under the law to which the parties have subjected it or, failing any indication thereon, under the law for the time being in force; or (b) that the party making the application was not given proper notice of the appointment of an arbitrator or of the arbitral proceedings or was otherwise unable to present his case; or (c) that the award contains decisions on matters beyond the scope of the agreement or the submission to arbitration, except that the arbitrator may decide a matter not contemplated by the submission or contained outside the submission if such decision is necessary for the fair resolution of the dispute; or (d) that the composition of the arbitral tribunal or the arbitral procedure was not in accordance with the agreement of the parties, unless such agreement was in conflict with a provision of this Part from which the parties cannot derogate; or (e) that the award has not yet become binding on the parties, or has been set aside or suspended by a court of competent jurisdiction within the country in which, or under the law of which, that award was made.

Under Section 33, the arbitral tribunal may, on its own motion or on an application by a party, correct any error in the award or make an additional award on any matter not decided in the award. The application for correction or additional award shall be made within thirty days of the receipt of the award, and the arbitral tribunal may correct any error or make an additional award within thirty days from the date of receipt of the application.

Under Section 34, an application for the setting aside of an arbitral award may be made to the court by a party to the arbitration within three months from the date on which the party making the application received the arbitral award. The court may entertain the application after the expiry of the said period of three months if it is satisfied that the applicant was prevented by sufficient cause from making the application within the said period.

Under Section 35, the court shall, on an application made by any party to the arbitration, order the arbitral tribunal to make an award within a time specified by the court.
Section 120 provides that when the Court has to form an opinion as to the contents of any document, other than a document produced in evidence in the case, the Court may either take notice of the contents of such document without proof, or may require evidence to be given of the contents of such document, as it thinks fit.

Section 121 provides that when the Court has to form an opinion as to the meaning of any written or printed word or symbol, the Court may either take notice of that meaning without proof, or may require evidence to be given of that meaning, as it thinks fit.

Section 122 provides that the Court shall take judicial notice of the following facts, namely:

(a) the day of the week on which any day of the year falls;

(b) the month in which any day of the year falls;

(c) the date of any public Act, Ordinance, Regulation, Notification, or other official document;

(d) the contents of any published document declared by this Act to be admissible in evidence;

(e) the signatures of any well-known person, or of any person in public or private service, or of any person in the regular course of business, or of any person in any professional capacity;

(f) the contents of any document forming part of the records of any Court, or of any public office or registry, or of any public or local fund, or of any Court, public office, registry or fund in any foreign country;

(g) the fact that any document is registered, or that any person is registered as the owner, executor, administrator or assignee of any property, or as the proprietor of any patent or trademark, or as the holder of any license, or as a member of any chamber of commerce or of any trade association;

(h) the identity and genuineness of handwriting, signatures, seals and finger impressions;

(i) the general course of nature;

(j) the course of business;

(k) the situation and extent of any country, state, city, town, village or other place;

(l) the boundaries and areas of any electoral constituency;

