Section 28K states that nothing in Sections 28A to 28J shall affect the application of any provisions of the Constitution to the High Court of Meghalaya, Manipur, and Tripura. The provisions of these sections shall have effect subject to any provision that may be made by any Legislature or other authority having power to make such provisions.

Section 29 states that on and from the appointed day, the common High Court shall have all such jurisdiction, powers, and authority as under the law in force immediately before the appointed day, are exercisable in respect of those territories by the High Court of Assam and Nagaland or the Court of the Judicial Commissioner for Manipur or the Court of the Judicial Commissioner for Tripura, as the case may be.

Section 30 abolishes the Courts of the Judicial Commissioners for Manipur and Tripura on and from the appointed day, while Section 31 establishes the principal seat of the common High Court at the same place as the principal seat of the High Court of Assam and Nagaland. The President may provide for the establishment of a permanent bench or benches of the common High Court at one or more places within the territories to which the jurisdiction of the High Court extends, other than the principal seat of the High Court.

Section 32 extends the jurisdiction of the common High Court to the Union territories of Mizoram and Arunachal Pradesh, while Section 33 allocates the expenditure in respect of salaries and allowances of the Judges of the common High Court amongst the States of Arunachal Pradesh, Mizoram, and Nagaland and the Union.

Section 34 contains special provisions relating to advocates and the Bar Council. It provides that any person who is an advocate entitled to practise in the High Court of Assam and Nagaland or in the Court of the Judicial Commissioner for Manipur or in the Court of the Judicial Commissioner for Tripura shall be entitled to practise as an advocate in the common High Court. The right of audience in the common High Court shall be regulated in accordance with the principles in force with respect to the right of audience in the High Court of Assam and Nagaland. The provisions of the Advocates Act, 1961, shall have effect in respect of the roll of the Bar Council of Assam, Nagaland, Meghalaya, Manipur, and Tripura subject to the modifications specified in the Act.

Overall, the Act aims to streamline the judicial process and ensure the smooth transfer of proceedings, orders, and jurisdiction to the High Courts of Meghalaya, Manipur, and Tripura. It also provides for the interpretation and application of certain provisions of the Constitution and the Advocates Act to these High Courts.
