The document also explains the fundamental right of constitutional remedies under Article 32 of the Indian Constitution. This article guarantees the right to move the Supreme Court for the enforcement of fundamental rights. The Supreme Court has the power to issue appropriate orders, directions, or writs, including habeas corpus, mandamus, prohibition, quo warranto, and certiorari, for the enforcement of fundamental rights. The right guaranteed by this article cannot be suspended except as otherwise provided for by the Constitution.

The document further explains the concept of habeas corpus, mandamus, prohibition, quo warranto, and certiorari writs. The writ of habeas corpus is used to ensure the release of a person who has been unlawfully detained. The writ of mandamus is used to direct a public official or authority to perform a duty they are legally bound to perform. The writ of prohibition is used to prevent a lower court or tribunal from exceeding its jurisdiction. The writ of quo warranto is used to question the authority of a person holding a public office. The writ of certiorari is used to quash an order or decision made by a lower court or tribunal.

In summary, the document explains the content of the Constitution (Forty-Third Amendment) Act, 1977, specifically the omission of Article 31D, and the fundamental right of constitutional remedies under Article 32 of the Indian Constitution. It also explains the concept of habeas corpus, mandamus, prohibition, quo warranto, and certiorari writs.
Summary:
The document discusses various aspects of Indian laws related to writs, specifically Certiorari and Quo Warranto. Certiorari is a writ issued by a higher court to a lower court or tribunal to quash an order passed without jurisdiction or in excess of jurisdiction. It is not available against equal or higher courts. Quo Warranto, on the other hand, is a writ issued to inquire into the legality of a person's claim to a public office. The document also discusses the power of Parliament to modify the rights conferred by Part III of the Constitution in their application to Armed Forces, Forces charged with the maintenance of public order, employees of intelligence or counterintelligence bureaus, and employees of telecommunication systems related to any Force, bureau, or organization. Additionally, the document discusses the suspension of the right to move the Supreme Court under Article 32 during a proclamation of Emergency, except for the fundamental rights guaranteed under Articles 20 and 21. Lastly, the document mentions the power of Parliament to make laws for prescribing punishment for acts declared to be offences under Part III of the Constitution. The document also refers to the now-repealed Article 32A, which prohibited the Supreme Court from considering the constitutional validity of any State law during writ proceedings to enforce Fundamental Rights. The document also mentions the now-repealed Article 370, which granted special status to the state of Jammu and Kashmir, allowing it to define permanent residents and confer special rights and privileges on them.
