The Indian government has made significant changes to the legal status of Jammu and Kashmir by rendering Article 370 inoperative, despite it still existing in the Constitution. This was done through a Presidential Order, C.O. 272; the Constitution (Application to Jammu and Kashmir) Order, 2019, which superseded the Constitution (Application to Jammu and Kashmir) Order, 1954. This effectively meant that the separate Constitution of Jammu and Kashmir was inoperative, and a single constitution now applies to all Indian states. The President used the third clause of Article 370, which allows for the article to be declared inoperative with exceptions and modifications, if recommended by the (non-existent) state constituent assembly. To circumvent the legal issue of the non-existent state constituent assembly, the President used the first clause of Article 370, which gives him the power to modify the Indian Constitution on subjects related to Jammu and Kashmir. The President added a new clause to Article 367, which deals with the interpretation of the Constitution, replacing the phrase 'Constituent Assembly of the State' with 'Legislative Assembly of the State.' Since the state legislative assembly has been suspended, the order says that any reference to the legislative assembly will be construed as a reference to the Governor of Jammu and Kashmir, who is an appointee of the Central government. This effectively means that the Indian Parliament now functions for the state legislative assembly.

The Constitution (One Hundred and First Amendment) Act, 2016 introduces the goods and services tax (GST) in India. This tax aims to replace various indirect taxes levied by the Union and State Governments and create a common national market for goods and services. The GST will be levied on all transactions involving the supply of goods or services or both, except those explicitly excluded.

The Act proposes to subsume various Central indirect taxes, such as Central Excise Duty, Additional Excises Duties, Excise Duty on medicinal and toilet preparations, Service Tax, Countervailing Duty, Special Additional Duty of Customs, and Central Surcharges and Cesses related to the supply of goods and services. Similarly, State Value Added Tax/Sales Tax, Entertainment Tax, Central Sales Tax, Octroi and Entry tax, Purchase Tax, Luxury tax, Taxes on lottery, betting and gambling, and State cesses and surcharges related to supply of goods and services will also be subsumed.

The Act also introduces Integrated Goods and Services Tax on inter-State transactions of goods and services. An additional tax of up to 1% on the supply of goods in the course of inter-State trade or commerce will be levied for a period of two years, collected by the Government of India, and assigned to the States from where the supply originates.

The Act confers concurrent powers upon Parliament and State Legislatures to make laws governing goods and services tax, covering all goods and services, except alcoholic liquor for human consumption. Petroleum and petroleum products will not be subject to the levy of Goods and Services Tax till a date notified on the recommendation of the Goods and Services Tax Council.

The Act provides compensation to the States for loss of revenue arising on account of implementing the Goods and Services Tax for a period that may extend to five years. A Goods and Services Tax Council is created to examine issues relating to goods and services tax and make recommendations to the Union and the States on parameters like rates, exemption list, and threshold limits. The Council will function under the Chairmanship of the Union Finance Minister and will have the Union Minister of State in charge of Revenue or Finance as a member, along with the Minister in-charge of Finance or Taxation or any other Minister nominated by each State Government. The Council's decisions will be taken by a majority of not less than three-fourths of the weighted votes of the members present and voting.

The Act provides transitional provisions to address any inconsistency arising with respect to any law relating to tax on goods or services or on both in force in any State on the commencement of the provisions of the Constitution as amended by this Act within a period of one year.
Incorporation of certain definitions in article 366.-

In article 366 of the Constitution, after clause (29), the following clause shall be inserted, namely:-

"(29A) 'tax on the sale or purchase of goods' includes-

(i) a tax on the transfer for consideration of controlled commodities;

(ii) a tax on the transfer of property in goods involved in the execution of a works contract;

(iii) a tax on delivery of goods on hire-purchase or any system of payment by instalments;

(iv) a tax on the transfer of the right to use any goods for any purpose for cash, deferred payment or other valuable consideration;

(v) a tax on the supply of goods by an unincorporated association or body of persons to a member thereof for cash, deferred payment or other valuable consideration;

(vi) a tax on the supply, by way of or as part of any service, of food or any drink for cash, deferred payment or other valuable consideration;".

Insertion of new entry in the Union List in the Seventh Schedule.-

In the Seventh Schedule to the Constitution, in the Union List, after entry 92A, the following entry shall be inserted, namely:-

"92B. Taxes on the consignment of goods (whether the consignment is to the person making the consignment or to any other person) where such consignment takes place in the course of inter-State trade or commerce."

Amendment of article 269.-

In article 269 of the Constitution,-

(a) in clause (1), for the words "on the sale or purchase of goods", the words "on the consignment of goods in the course of inter-State trade or commerce" shall be substituted;

(b) in clause (3), after the words "by law", the words "for determining when a consignment of goods takes place in the course of inter-State trade or commerce" shall be inserted;

(c) in clause (4), for the words "on the sale or purchase of goods", the words "on the consignment of goods in the course of inter-State trade or commerce" shall be substituted.

Amendment of article 286.-

In clause (3) of article 286 of the Constitution,-

(a) for the words "goods of the same class or kind", the words "goods" shall be substituted;

(b) for the words "such sale or purchase", the words "such transfer" shall be substituted;

(c) for the words "the sale or purchase of such goods", the words "the transfer of such goods" shall be substituted;

(d) for the words "the sale or purchase of goods", the words "the transfer of goods" shall be substituted;

(e) after the words "by law", the words "concerning the levy of tax on the transfer of goods involved in the execution of a works contract, on the delivery of goods on hire-purchase or any system of payment by instalments and on the right to use any goods" shall be inserted.

Validation of laws and collections.-

(1) Notwithstanding anything contained in the Constitution,-

(a) any law levying tax on the supply of food or drink for consideration and also the collection or recovery of tax made under any such law;

(b) any tax on the supply of food or drink for consideration levied and collected by any local authority in accordance with the provisions of any law for the time being in force in any State;

(c) any collection or recovery of tax made by any local authority under any such law;

shall be deemed to be and to have always been valid.

(2) Notwithstanding anything contained in the Constitution, no sales tax shall be payable on food or drink supplied by a hotelier to a person lodged in the hotel during the period commencing on the 1st day of January, 1976 and ending with the date on which the Constitution (Forty-sixth Amendment) Act, 1982 receives the assent of the President if the conditions mentioned in sub-clause (2) of clause (6) of the Constitution (Forty-sixth Amendment) Act, 1982 are satisfied.

(3) Notwithstanding anything contained in the Constitution, no sales tax shall be payable on food or drink supplied by restaurants during the period commencing on the date of the judgment of the Supreme Court in Northern India Caterers (India) Limited case and ending with the date on which the Constitution (Forty-sixth Amendment) Act, 1982 receives the assent of the President.

Power of President to remove difficulties.-

(1) If any difficulty arises in giving effect to the provisions of the Constitution (Forty-sixth Amendment) Act, 1982, the President may, by order, make such provisions, not inconsistent with the provisions of the Constitution, as appear to him to be necessary or expedient for the removal of the difficulty:

Provided that no such order shall be made after the expiration of two years from the date on which the Constitution (Forty-sixth Amendment) Act, 1982 receives the assent of the President.

The Guardians and Wards Act, 1890, is an Act that consolidates and amends the law relating to guardians and wards. The Act extends to the whole of India, except the State of Jammu and Kashmir. The Act is divided into four chapters, with Chapter I being the preliminary chapter, Chapter II dealing with the appointment and declaration of guardians, Chapter III outlining the duties, rights, and liabilities of guardians, and Chapter IV containing supplemental provisions.

Under Chapter II, the Act provides for the appointment and declaration of guardians by the Court. The Court may make an order appointing or declaring a guardian of the person or property, or both, of a minor if it is satisfied that it is for the welfare of the minor. The Court may also remove any guardian who has not been appointed by will or other instrument or appointed or declared by the Court. The Act specifies the persons who are entitled to apply for an order, including the person desirous of being, or claiming to be, the guardian of the minor, any relative or friend of the minor, the Collector of the district or other local area where the minor ordinarily resides or in which he has property, or the Collector having authority with respect to the class to which the minor belongs. The Court having jurisdiction to entertain the application is determined by whether the application is with respect to the guardianship of the person or property of the minor.

Chapter III of the Act outlines the duties, rights, and liabilities of guardians. A guardian stands in a fiduciary relation to his ward and must not make any profit out of his office. The fiduciary relation of the guardian to his ward extends to and affects purchases by the guardian of the property of the ward and by the ward of the property of the guardian. A guardian of the property of a ward is bound to deal with the property as carefully as a man of ordinary prudence would deal with his own property. The Act also specifies the powers and limitations of guardians of property appointed or declared by the Court.

Chapter IV of the Act contains supplemental provisions, including provisions for the enforcement of orders made under the Act, the penalty for removal of a ward from the jurisdiction of the Court, the penalty for contumacy, reports by Collectors and subordinate Courts, the finality of orders, costs, and the power of the High Court to make rules.

In summary, the Guardians and Wards Act, 1890, is an Act that consolidates and amends the law relating to guardians and wards in India. The Act provides for the appointment and declaration of guardians by the Court, outlines the duties, rights, and liabilities of guardians, and contains supplemental provisions for the enforcement of orders made under the Act.
